---
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro'
import '@/styles/global.css'

const pageTitle = 'Export & Quality Guide | Splicer'
const pageDescription =
  'How Splicer exports video: when exporting re-encodes, what WebM and the quality presets actually do, how Original copies without loss, and how to hit a file size.'
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <main id="main" class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-3xl flex-col gap-8 px-4 py-8 md:py-12">
    <article class="flex flex-col gap-6">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          Export &amp; quality guide
        </h1>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
          A quick guide to what happens when you export, why a WebM file is not automatically
          smaller or higher quality, and how to keep as much quality as possible.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 rounded-lg border border-slate-200/70 bg-slate-50/60 p-4 dark:border-slate-700/60 dark:bg-slate-900/40">
        <p class="font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          Exporting to WebM always re-encodes your video, which loses a little quality:
        </p>
        <ul class="flex flex-col gap-1.5 pl-5 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            Your source is already lossy. Its original codec (for example the H.264 in an MP4)
            discarded data when the video was first encoded.
          </li>
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            Exporting to WebM decodes the video back to raw pixels, then re-encodes them with VP8 or
            VP9. That second encode is also lossy. This is called generation loss, like photocopying
            a photocopy.
          </li>
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            Re-encoding always loses a little quality, and H.264 cannot live in a WebM container, so
            there is no zero-loss path from an MP4 to WebM.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          Exporting usually re-encodes your video
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          With a quality preset, Splicer decodes your video back to raw frames and encodes them
          again from scratch. Your source file is already compressed (and that first compression
          already discarded some detail), so this second encode is a fresh round of compression on
          top of it. The one exception is <span class="font-medium">Original (no re-encode)</span>,
          which copies the compressed video as it is.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          WebM is a container, not a codec
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          A &ldquo;.webm&rdquo; file is just a box. The efficiency comes from the codec inside it,
          which for WebM is VP8 or VP9. Choosing WebM does not by itself make a file smaller or
          better. What matters is which codec you use and how it is configured.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          Why &ldquo;smaller and lossless&rdquo; is not possible when re-encoding
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          Re-encoding an already-compressed video is like photocopying a photocopy: each pass
          loses a little, an effect called generation loss. You cannot add back detail that was
          already thrown away, so a re-encode can never increase quality. Getting a smaller file
          with truly zero quality loss is not achievable by re-encoding. Original avoids the loss by
          not re-encoding at all, which also means it keeps the source size; Resolution and Target
          size make a file smaller by re-encoding it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          Is WebM actually more efficient?
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          It depends entirely on the codec, not the container:
        </p>
        <ul class="flex flex-col gap-1.5 pl-5 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-medium">VP8</span> is roughly the same generation as the H.264 found
            in most MP4s, so it is often no smaller (and can be larger) than your source.
          </li>
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-medium">VP9</span> is meaningfully more efficient, comparable to
            H.265 / HEVC, and can produce smaller files at similar visual quality.
          </li>
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-medium">AV1</span> is more efficient still, but is out of scope for
            this app.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          So a smaller file generally means VP9, applied to a source that was not already encoded
          very efficiently, and accepting a small amount of generation loss.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          What the quality presets do
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          The Maximum, High, Medium, and Low presets set the target bitrate relative to your
          source, scaled to the output size: Maximum aims up to 1.5&times; the source bitrate,
          High about the same as the source, Medium about 60%, and Low about 35%. When the source
          bitrate cannot be measured, they fall back to fixed bitrates for the output size.
          &ldquo;Maximum&rdquo; is not mathematically lossless. If your goal is to keep every pixel
          identical, use Original instead.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          Original (no re-encode)
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          Original copies the source video's compressed frames straight into the new file, so
          nothing is lost and the export takes seconds. Compressed video stores a full picture only
          at key frames, and a copy has to start on one, so each cut moves back to the key frame
          before it. The export panel lists how far each cut moves and the timeline marks it with
          a dashed line. Audio is re-encoded to stay in sync. Original works in Basic mode when
          every clip on the timeline was encoded the same way, nothing is cropped, FPS and
          Resolution are on Original, and the format can hold the source codec: MP4, MOV, or MKV
          for H.264 and HEVC, WebM or MKV for VP9 and VP8.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          Resolution and Target size
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          Resolution caps the shorter side of the output (1080p, 720p, 480p, or 360p) and never
          scales up. Fewer pixels need fewer bits, which is the most reliable way to shrink a file
          the encoder cannot compress further at full size. Target size aims the whole file a
          little under the size you pick. When that leaves too few bits for a clear picture, it
          lowers the resolution, then the frame rate, and the panel tells you what it chose.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          How to keep quality as high as possible
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          Pick <span class="font-medium">Original (no re-encode)</span>: it keeps every frame as it
          was. When Original is unavailable, export to the
          <span class="font-medium">same format as your source</span>, at
          <span class="font-medium">Maximum</span> quality, with FPS left on
          <span class="font-medium">Original</span>. That re-encodes, so it is not a perfect copy,
          but Maximum aims above the source bitrate to keep generation loss small. Changing the
          format, lowering the quality, or reducing the FPS increases the amount of quality lost.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          Will exporting make my file smaller?
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          Usually, if you ask for it. High aims at the source bitrate, and Medium and Low aim
          below it, so they come out about the size of the source or smaller. VP9 needs fewer bits
          than H.264 for the same quality, and the presets account for that. The export panel
          shows an estimated size with a likely range next to your source size. One limit: the
          browser encoder cannot go below a minimum bitrate for a given resolution, so a source
          that is already very compressed (common for screen recordings) can come out larger at
          any quality. The panel says so when that happens.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2 rounded-lg border border-slate-200/70 bg-slate-50/60 p-4 dark:border-slate-700/60 dark:bg-slate-900/40">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">Recommendations</h2>
        <ul class="flex flex-col gap-1.5 pl-5 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            Want the highest quality? Use Original (no re-encode), or Maximum quality in the same
            format as your source when Original is unavailable.
          </li>
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            Want a smaller file that looks the same? Use WebM with VP9 and accept a small,
            usually invisible, amount of generation loss.
          </li>
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            Avoid VP8 if your goal is a smaller file; it rarely beats the H.264 in a typical MP4.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
